This is strongly enforced in human tournament play.
It's important in endgames when a position may or
may not be winnable, and when the players themselves
may or may not know if it's winnable.  Arbitration
is thus effectively decided by whether one side can
make any progress within 50 moves.

For instance, I know that if I'm defending with a
lone king against a king and two knights, I can't
lose (provided I don't make a mistake I know I never
would).  My opponent may not know this, or may not
believe I won't make such a mistake, so we play out
fifty moves at which point it's a draw.

On the other hand, an amateur may not know how to
mate with a lone rook.  Rather than waiting potentially
years for him to stumble upon it, he gets 50 moves to
figure it out.  Bishop and knight is much harder, and
even strong players may not know how to do it, so
there's a greater chance of the 50 moves running out
there.

